summary:
Aeolus, by team Data May Cry, is a web app for the NASA Space Apps 2026 “Earth System Trend Detective” challenge. It answers a simple question for any place on Earth: is the climate really changing here, or is it just noise?

Users pick a continent, country or province on a 3D globe. Aeolus then tests forty-five years of NASA MERRA-2 data, from 1980 to 2025, across nine variables, including temperature, humidity, rainfall, soil moisture and snow. The Mann–Kendall test and Sen’s slope label each trend as clear, likely or unclear, with a 95% confidence range, shown per decade or as total change.

The side panel works as a compact menu. It covers the following:

the trend itself;
a climate fingerprint linking all nine variables;
similar places worldwide;
custom charts with seasonal comparisons and month-by-month trends.

Time travel replays yearly anomalies across the globe.

Results can be exported as PDF reports, CSV files or shareable links. Aeolus is bilingual (English and Turkish), works on mobile and offers a public API.
